  "name": "@calltelemetry/ct-lab-mcp",
  "readme": "# @calltelemetry/ct-lab-mcp\n\n> **Model Context Protocol (MCP) Server for Unified Call Telemetry Lab, Fleet, and Appliance Management across Proxmox VE and VMware ESXi**\n\n[![NPM Version](https://img.shields.io/npm/v/@calltelemetry/ct-lab-mcp.svg)](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@calltelemetry/ct-lab-mcp)\n[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-blue.svg)](LICENSE)\n[![TypeScript](https://img.shields.io/badge/TypeScript-5.7+-blue.svg)](https://www.typescriptlang.org/)\n[![Node Version](https://img.shields.io/badge/node-%3E%3D20.0.0-brightgreen.svg)](https://nodejs.org/)\n\n`@calltelemetry/ct-lab-mcp` is the single public MCP fa\u00e7ade for Call Telemetry lab operations. It exposes high-level multi-hypervisor management (Proxmox VE & VMware ESXi), disposable VM workflows, and secure appliance diagnostics while delegating canonical asset identity, lease ownership, and release-line policy to the private `lab-inventory` CLI supplied by `ct-meta`.\n\n---\n\n## Architecture\n\n```\nAI Clients (Claude Desktop / Cursor / Web / AI Agents)\n                  \u2502\n                  \u25bc\n       [MCP Server / Transports]\n       \u251c\u2500\u2500 Stdio Transport (stdout JSON-RPC, stderr SafeLogger)\n       \u2514\u2500\u2500 Express SSE Transport (/sse, /messages, /health)\n                  \u2502\n                  \u25bc\n         [MCP Tool Registry]\n  \u251c\u2500\u2500 list_hosts          (Proxmox + ESXi capacity, health & 8GB slot quotas)\n  \u251c\u2500\u2500 list_inventory      (Unified VM fleet inventory with Linear tags)\n  \u251c\u2500\u2500 claim_slot          (8GB slot leases with TTL & Linear issue tracking)\n  \u251c\u2500\u2500 release_slot        (Release lease + optional automated VM destruction)\n  \u251c\u2500\u2500 provision_vm        (Disposable VM provisioning on Proxmox/ESXi)\n  \u251c\u2500\u2500 proxmox_quick_provision (Size-driven randomized Proxmox fleet plan/apply)\n  \u251c\u2500\u2500 pin_pool_slots      (Wraps pin-pool-slots.sh: pin web SHA tag on guest IPs)\n  \u251c\u2500\u2500 vm_action           (Power lifecycle with non-bypassable safety guardrails)\n  \u251c\u2500\u2500 host_maintenance_shutdown (Guarded whole-host drain and graceful shutdown)\n  \u251c\u2500\u2500 lab_mission         (Canonical manifest-bound multi-phase mission)\n  \u2514\u2500\u2500 appliance_exec      (Mediated cli.sh commands over SSH:2222 with redaction)\n                  \u2502\n  \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u253c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n  \u25bc               \u25bc               \u25bc                   \u25bc\n[MultiHypervisor] [Slot Engine]   [Safety Guardrails] [Appliance SSH]\n  \u251c\u2500\u2500 Proxmox VE   \u251c\u2500\u2500 ProxmoxLab  \u251c\u2500\u2500 Proxmox fixtures \u251c\u2500\u2500 ssh2 port 2222\n  \u2502   (2x8GB)      \u2502   (2x8GB max) \u2502   (100-104, 9000s) \u251c\u2500\u2500 cli.sh / db vacuum\n  \u2514\u2500\u2500 VMware ESXi  \u251c\u2500\u2500 ProxmoxMini \u2514\u2500\u2500 ESXi fixtures    \u251c\u2500\u2500 migrate / logs\n      (8x8GB)      \u2502   (4x8GB max)     (CUCM, CUBE,     \u2514\u2500\u2500 Redaction Engine\n                   \u2514\u2500\u2500 ESXi (8x8GB)     Runners, K8s)\n                          \u2502\n                          \u25bc\n            [Lab Credential Resolver]\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500 Tier 1: Process Environment (LAB_PROXMOX_*, GOVC_*, LAB_VM_*)\n            \u251c\u2500\u2500 Tier 2: Doppler REST API v3 (DOPPLER_TOKEN)\n            \u2514\u2500\u2500 Tier 3: In-Memory Mock Fallback / Offline Dev\n```\n\nIn a governed `ct-meta` installation there is only one registered MCP server:\n`@calltelemetry/ct-lab-mcp`. The Python `lab-inventory` program remains a\nprivate canonical policy engine behind the Node fa\u00e7ade; it is not registered as\na second public MCP. Set `CT_LAB_CANONICAL_REQUIRED=1` to fail closed rather\nthan use the legacy Node VMID/name slot state when the bridge is unavailable.\n\n---\n\n## Key Features\n\n- **Multi-Hypervisor Fleet Management**: Polymorphic abstraction unifying Proxmox VE clusters (`proxmox-lab`, `proxmox-mini`) and VMware ESXi hosts (`esxi-intel-192-168-123-176`).\n- **Disposable VM Provisioning**: Give `proxmox_quick_provision` one resource size (`memoryMb`, `vcpus`, `diskGb`). Live capacity filters the homogeneous Proxmox pool, then one eligible host is selected with system randomness. Apply repeats the reviewed explicit host, VMID, and operation ID.\n- **Global 8GB Slot Allocation Engine**:\n  - `proxmox-lab`: Strictly capped at **2 concurrent 8GB slots** (16 GB allocated, 16 GB reserved for CUCM & core fixtures).\n  - `proxmox-mini`: Strictly capped at **4 concurrent 8GB slots** (32 GB allocated).\n  - `esxi-intel-192-168-123-176`: Managed candidate slot ranking with **8 concurrent 8GB slots** and datastore space checks.\n- **Non-Bypassable Safety Guardrails**: Hard protection preventing destructive actions (`destroy`, `stop`, `reboot`) against core infrastructure:\n  - Cisco Unified Communications Manager (CUCM 14 / 15 publishers and subscribers)\n  - Cisco CUBE Voice Gateways / Cat8000v\n  - GitHub Actions CI Runners (`GH-Runner`)\n  - Lab Kubernetes Clusters (`LAB-K8S`)\n  - VM Templates (VMIDs `9000`\u2013`9999` or template flags)\n  - SPA-1103 on minis VMID `182`, including cleanup of expired or untracked rows\n- **Physical Maintenance Shutdown**: `host_maintenance_shutdown` accepts one\n  exact canonical Proxmox or ESXi host. It plans by default; apply requires the\n  reviewed plan digest and exact host confirmation, preflights credentials,\n  gracefully drains catalog-matched unclaimed guests, shuts CUCM subscribers\n  before publishers, and refuses active claims, partial discovery, identity\n  drift, clustered Proxmox, or an ESXi maintenance-mode failure. Force host\n  power-off is not implemented.\n- **Remote Appliance CLI Control**: Mediated execution of `cli.sh` commands over SSH (port 2222) for status inspection, health diagnostics, Ecto migrations, table vacuums, and container logs.\n- **Dynamic Secret Redaction**: Real-time masking prevents passwords, Doppler API tokens, and private keys from leaking into MCP responses or stderr logs.\n- **Dual Transports**: Clean standard I/O transport (`stdio`) and Express HTTP/SSE transport (`sse`) with `/health`, `/sse`, and `/messages` endpoints.\n- **3-Tier Credential Resolution**:\n  1. *Tier 1*: Direct Environment Variables (`LAB_PROXMOX_*`, `GOVC_*`, `LAB_VM_CREDENTIALS_*`)\n  2. *Tier 2*: Doppler Secrets REST API v3 (`DOPPLER_TOKEN`)\n  3. *Tier 3*: In-Memory Mock Hypervisor & Appliance Fallback (for offline test suites)\n\n---\n\n## Installation\n\n```bash\n# Global installation\nnpm install -g @calltelemetry/ct-lab-mcp\n\n# Or run directly with npx\nnpx @calltelemetry/ct-lab-mcp\n```\n\nMerges to `main` publish automatically. If `package.json` version is not on npm,\nthat version is published, tagged, and released. If it is already on npm, CI\nbumps the patch version, publishes, tags, and creates the GitHub Release.\n`--dry-run` / docs-only work that should not ship must not land on `main`, or\nbump the version only when the package should ship.\n\n```bash\n# Current published line\nnpm view @calltelemetry/ct-lab-mcp version\n```\n\n---\n\n## CLI Usage\n\n```bash\nct-lab-mcp [options]\n```\n\n### Command-Line Options\n\n| Flag | Description | Default | Environment Variable |\n|------|-------------|---------|----------------------|\n| `-t, --transport <type>` | Transport mode (`stdio` or `sse`) | `stdio` | `CT_LAB_MCP_TRANSPORT` |\n| `-p, --port <number>` | HTTP port for SSE transport | `3000` | `PORT` |\n| `-H, --host <string>` | Host interface to bind SSE server | `127.0.0.1` | `HOST` |\n| `-c, --config <path>` | Path to optional JSON configuration file | *None* | *None* |\n| `--doppler-token <token>` | Doppler config-scoped service token (prefer the environment variable) | *None* | `DOPPLER_TOKEN` |\n| `--doppler-project <name>`| Doppler project name | *None* | `DOPPLER_PROJECT` |\n| `--doppler-config <name>` | Doppler environment / config | *None* | `DOPPLER_CONFIG` |\n| *None* | Bound Doppler API resolution; invalid values use 8 seconds, valid values clamp to 250-30000 ms | `8000` | `CT_LAB_MCP_RESOLVE_TIMEOUT_MS` |\n| `--force-mock` | Force in-memory mock hypervisor & appliance mode | `false` | `CT_LAB_MOCK_MODE` |\n| `--redact-secrets` | Enforce strict secret redaction on all logs | `true` | *None* |\n| `preflight --asset-id <id> --linear-issue <key>` | Validate canonical inventory, exact VM identity, and release-line qualification before guest commands | n/a | `CT_LAB_CANONICAL_CLI`, `LAB_INVENTORY_FILE`, `CT_LAB_CANONICAL_REQUIRED` |\n| `fleet versions --asset-id <id>` | Qualify exact canonical assets against their cut release | n/a | `CT_LAB_CANONICAL_CLI`, `CT_LAB_CANONICAL_REQUIRED` |\n| `fleet candidates --memory-mb <mb> --vcpus <n> --disk-gb <gb>` | Rank reusable VMs and host capacity; all three capacity requirements are mandatory | n/a | `CT_LAB_CANONICAL_CLI`, `CT_LAB_CANONICAL_REQUIRED` |\n| `fleet group <group-id>` | Resolve a canonical fleet group without mutation | n/a | `CT_LAB_CANONICAL_CLI`, `CT_LAB_CANONICAL_REQUIRED` |\n| `-v, --version` | Output the current version | *None* | *None* |\n| `-h, --help` | Display CLI help text | *None* | *None* |\n\n### Examples\n\n```bash\n# Run over stdio (default for Claude Desktop and Cursor)\nct-lab-mcp\n\n# Run SSE server on port 3001\nct-lab-mcp --transport sse --port 3001 --host 0.0.0.0\n\n# Run with a config-scoped Doppler service token already exported by the caller\nDOPPLER_PROJECT=\"ai-workspace\" DOPPLER_CONFIG=\"dev\" ct-lab-mcp\n\n# Offline mock mode for development / unit testing\nct-lab-mcp --force-mock\n\n# Governed fixture qualification (the release is cut by ct-release; the lab\n# only qualifies the exact fixture against that release)\nct-lab-mcp preflight --asset-id proxmox-vm-uat-01 --linear-issue UAT-1202\n\n# Rank only Proxmox candidates that satisfy the appliance's capacity floor\nct-lab-mcp fleet candidates --memory-mb 8192 --vcpus 4 --disk-gb 200 --hypervisor proxmox\n```\n\n### Canonical lab mode\n\n`ct-meta` starts the public MCP with these environment variables:\n\n```text\nCT_LAB_CANONICAL_CLI=/path/to/lab-inventory\nCT_LAB_CANONICAL_REQUIRED=1\n```\n\nIn this mode `lab_preflight`, inventory and storage-capacity reads, claims,\nreleases, reclaim, provisioning, VM lifecycle, and inventory-bound CUBE IOS-XE\ncalls execute through the canonical CLI. The Node fa\u00e7ade never creates a\nsecond lease store and never treats a VMID, name, IP address, Linear key, or\noperation ID as ownership. Mutation calls require the canonical asset\nidentity, exact operation inputs, and the CLI's dry-run / apply receipt\nworkflow. Legacy guest, storage, snapshot, CUBE lease, and appliance mutations\nthat do not yet have an identity-bound bridge still return\n`CANONICAL_OPERATION_REQUIRES_BRIDGE`.\n\nCanonical host reads dispatch one identity-bound CLI discovery per requested\nhost. Multi-host reads keep successful host payloads and return\n`partial: true` with structured `discovery_unavailable` warnings for failed\npeers; an exact-host read, or a read where every requested host fails, remains\nfail-closed. `list_inventory` consumes the canonical\n`calltelemetry-lab-vm-inventory/v1` partial-discovery contract and may project\nits rows by exact host or hypervisor without creating another inventory. Exact\nhost reads invoke `vm inventory --host-asset-id`, hypervisor reads invoke\n`vm inventory --hypervisor`, and only all-scope reads invoke\n`vm inventory --all`.\nWarnings are preserved, with the current singleton ESXi warning bound to its\ncanonical host identity. Status, template, text, and tag projection remains\nunsupported until those fields have an authoritative canonical CLI contract.\n\n`list_storage` delegates to `lab-inventory host capacity --asset-id` once per\nrequested exact host. Its default `includeContent: false` returns normalized\npool/datastore capacity plus Proxmox pool content capabilities. `host: all`\npreserves healthy hosts and emits `partial: true` with structured unreachable\nwarnings; an exact-host failure or all-host failure remains fail-closed. The\ncanonical CLI does not currently prove individual ISO, VM-disk, or backup\nvolume contents, so `includeContent: true` returns\n`CANONICAL_DETAIL_UNSUPPORTED` without consulting legacy Node credentials.\nESXi datastore `enabled`, `shared`, and content-capability fields are returned\nas `null` because the current canonical capacity contract does not expose them.\n\n`lab_mission` is the bounded fa\u00e7ade for `lab-inventory mission run`. It accepts\nan immutable manifest path and canonical SHA-256, plans by default, and requires\nthe exact asset confirmation plus a clean registered executor checkout for\napply. Its internal mission deadline defaults to four hours and cannot exceed\nsix hours. The canonical CLI receives that deadline. The Node watchdog waits\nan additional fixed 30 seconds for the canonical MissionRunner to terminate\nits executor group, write its terminal receipt, restore state, and release its\nlock. If that grace expires, Node terminates only the CLI-owned process group\nand reports a hard timeout; it does not claim to discover or clean up executor\ngroups that deliberately created a separate session. Signal termination is\nalways reported as a nonzero failure with `signalCode` preserved. It has no\ncaller-selected discovery source, command name, or free-form argument input,\nand it stores no lease, mission, inventory, or receipt authority in the Node\nprocess. Package rollout therefore depends on the corresponding canonical\nMissionRunner deadline and cleanup implementation in ct-meta.\n\nCUBE control is `cube_list`, `cube_status`, `cube_exec`, `cube_config`,\n`cube_dialpeer_show`, `cube_dialpeer_apply`, `cube_xcc_enable`,\n`cube_xcc_status`, and `cube_ios_snapshot`. Power stays `vm_action` /\ncanonical `vm_power`. Claims stay `vm_claim`. Dial-peer apply is additive\nonly. Wipe-first `setup_cube_xcc` is not registered.\n\nClaiming policy is intentionally conservative: prefer a fresh unclaimed\nstopped candidate or a new disposable VM; reuse an active running VM only when\nthe current run proves the exact lease, coordinator ref, and live managed\nmetadata. Reclaim is permitted only for the oldest eligible expired claim when\nall eligible capacity is full. Protected and unknown assets are never\nreclaimed. A disposable claim that failed authentication may be destroyed and\nrecreated only after preserving the failure receipt.\n\nCanonical mutation parameters are deliberately explicit:\n\n- `claim_slot` and `provision_vm` require an exact `assetId`, active\n  `linearIssue`, and unique `operationId`; apply also requires matching\n  `confirmAssetId`.\n- `release_slot` requires the exact `leaseId` and an `artifactReceipt`.\n- `appliance_candidate_retire` is a separate, dry-run-default cleanup path\n  for an operator-approved failed disposable candidate. It requires the exact\n  asset, lease, failed candidate operation and immutable failure receipt;\n  canonical policy independently matches provisioning, live identity and shared\n  quarantine. Apply additionally confirms the asset, VMID and SMBIOS UUID.\n  Use separate unused plan/apply receipt paths. Failure records remain intact;\n  an expired guest is never renewed or reused, and verified absence precedes\n  ownership release. There is no force, host/IP selector or legacy fallback.\n- `purge_slots` delegates only to `claims gc`; age, force, orphan, and VM-name\n  shortcuts are rejected.\n- `list_expired_vms` classifies live VMs as legal, expiring, expired, dead\n  leftover, or protected. It never deletes; terminal Linear-ticket VMs are\n  classified (`dead_running` / `dead_stopped`) but ticket terminality alone\n  never makes a claim delete-ready (ADR 0400 lease precedence).\n- `cleanup_expired_vms` is read-only when `dryRun: true` (or `apply: false`):\n  omit `confirmAssets` for the full classification or provide an exact\n  comma-separated scope to preview selected and excluded rows. Mutation\n  requires a non-empty `confirmAssets` list and acts only on selected\n  delete-ready rows; a row is delete-ready only when its exact claim expiry\n  has passed (or it is a fully unclaimed leftover), so a running expired guest\n  receives an exact-identity graceful shutdown only after it is selected.\n  Invalid, duplicate, unknown, stale, non-delete-ready, ambiguous, or\n  identity-drift keys fail before the first mutation.\n  `deleteTerminalIssues: false` remains available to exclude the\n  terminal-ticket policy. Protected fixtures and non-terminal running\n  guests are never candidates.\n- `vm_renew` and `vm_transfer` preserve the exact asset and lease identity.\n  Running-guest renewal and expired-running transfer are accepted only when\n  the canonical CLI proves their narrow recovery conditions.\n- `vm_action` maps only to canonical asset-bound status, guest reboot, power,\n  and evidence-first delete operations.\n- `host_maintenance_shutdown` is separate from `vm_action`. It is dry-run by\n  default and routes only through canonical `lab-inventory host\n  maintenance-shutdown`; the public MCP never sends provider host commands\n  directly. Apply requires `confirmHostAssetId` and the latest `planDigest`.\n  A partial transaction remains fail-closed for manual reconciliation and is\n  never blindly redispatched with the same operation ID.\n- `proxmox_quick_provision` wraps `proxmox-quick-provision.sh`. Pass one `size`\n  object; `hostAssetId` defaults to `auto`, filters live Proxmox capacity, and\n  randomly selects among eligible hosts. Production randomness is not seeded;\n  `placementSeed` exists for reproducible tests. Apply requires the reviewed\n  explicit `hostAssetId`, `vmid`, and `operationId`. `0.8.8-stable` requires\n  `baseVersion` plus `webImage`; install uses the GCS base. Never VMID 182.\n- Expired leases with attached VMs remain occupied until the deletion transport\n  succeeds. Protected or failed deletions retain their exact lease and VM\n  identity, preventing phantom free capacity.\n- `pin_pool_slots` wraps `pin-pool-slots.sh` by tag+host. Default is plan-only;\n  `apply: true` pins `calltelemetry/web:<line>-stable-<12sha>` over SSH :2222.\n\n0.8.8 is SHA-channel. Do not invent `latest-0.8.8-stable.txt`. Point CUBE XCC\nat `http://<guest-ip>:80/api/xcc/<cube_id>`.\n\n---\n\n## MCP Tools Reference\n\nThe server registers 7 comprehensive tools conforming to the MCP JSON Schema specification:\n\n### 1. `list_hosts`\nQuery capacity, health, CPU/RAM/Disk metrics, and 8GB slot limits across Proxmox VE (`proxmox-lab`, `proxmox-mini`) and VMware ESXi (`esxi-intel-192-168-123-176`).\n\n```json\n{\n  \"host\": \"all\",              // \"proxmox-lab\" | \"proxmox-mini\" | \"esxi-intel-192-168-123-176\" | \"all\"\n  \"hypervisorType\": \"all\",    // \"proxmox\" | \"esxi\" | \"all\"\n  \"refresh\": false            // boolean: force live hypervisor API poll\n}\n```\n\n### 2. `list_inventory`\nQuery unified fleet inventory across Proxmox VE and VMware ESXi VMs with power status, IP addresses, VLAN, protected flags, release version, and Linear lease tags.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"hypervisor\": \"all\",        // \"proxmox\" | \"esxi\" | \"all\"\n  \"host\": \"proxmox-mini\",     // optional host filter\n  \"status\": \"all\",            // \"all\" | \"running\" | \"stopped\" | \"claimed\" | \"disposable\"\n  \"includeTemplates\": false,  // boolean (default: false)\n  \"filter\": \"0.8.7-stable\",   // text search across name, ID, IP, Linear issue, tags\n  \"tags\": [\"uat\", \"disposable\"]\n}\n```\n\n### 3. `claim_slot`\nLease an 8GB appliance slot on a specified host or randomly across eligible Proxmox peers, with lease TTL and Linear issue tracking. Use `placementSeed` only in deterministic tests.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"tag\": \"CTUAT-693\",         // required string: issue tag or session identifier\n  \"linearIssue\": \"CTUAT-693\", // Linear issue key\n  \"host\": \"auto\",             // \"proxmox-lab\" | \"proxmox-mini\" | \"esxi-intel-192-168-123-176\" | \"auto\"\n  \"hypervisorType\": \"any\",    // \"proxmox\" | \"esxi\" | \"any\"\n  \"durationMinutes\": 60,      // integer (1 - 1440 minutes / 24 hours, default: 60)\n  \"purpose\": \"UAT Testing\"    // optional description\n}\n```\n\n*Enforces strict capacity quotas: Attempting to claim a 3rd slot on `proxmox-lab` or a 5th slot on `proxmox-mini` is strictly rejected.*\n\n### 4. `release_slot`\nRelease an active 8GB slot claim by `claimId`, `tag`, `requester`, or `linearIssue`, with optional automatic destruction of attached test VMs.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"claimId\": \"claim-a1b2c3d4\", // optional string: specific claim ID\n  \"linearIssue\": \"CTUAT-693\",  // optional string: release all claims matching Linear issue\n  \"destroyVm\": true            // boolean: automatically destroy attached test VM\n}\n```\n\n### 5. `provision_vm`\nProvision a new disposable Call Telemetry appliance VM on Proxmox VE or VMware ESXi from a base template with IP/VLAN networking and auto-attached 8GB slot lease.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"sourceVmIdOrName\": 9000,                  // template VMID or template name\n  \"newVmName\": \"ct-appliance-uat-105\",       // name for the new VM\n  \"targetHost\": \"proxmox-mini\",              // optional host (auto-placed if omitted)\n  \"vlan\": 124,                               // optional VLAN tag\n  \"ipAddress\": \"192.168.124.105\",            // optional static IP\n  \"linearIssue\": \"CTUAT-693\",                // Linear issue tag\n  \"startOnDeploy\": true                      // power on immediately after cloning\n}\n```\n\n### Size-driven Proxmox fleet plan\n\n```json\n{\n  \"linearIssue\": \"REL-472\",\n  \"version\": \"0.8.7-rc438\",\n  \"purpose\": \"fleet-puppet\",\n  \"hostAssetId\": \"auto\",\n  \"size\": {\n    \"memoryMb\": 12288,\n    \"vcpus\": 6,\n    \"diskGb\": 160\n  },\n  \"apply\": false\n}\n```\n\nThe plan returns `placement.selected_host_asset_id`, the complete eligible host\nset, `target.vmid`, and `operation_id`. Copy those exact identities into apply;\ndo not leave `hostAssetId` as `auto` for a mutating call.\n\n### 6. `vm_action`\nExecute power lifecycle operations across Proxmox and ESXi with non-bypassable guardrails protecting core infrastructure.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"vmIdOrName\": \"ct-appliance-uat-105\",     // VMID or VM name/path\n  \"host\": \"proxmox-mini\",                   // target host name\n  \"action\": \"start\",                        // \"start\" | \"stop\" | \"reboot\" | \"shutdown\" | \"reset\" | \"status\" | \"destroy\"\n  \"force\": false                            // boolean: force immediate action (guardrails cannot be bypassed)\n}\n```\n\n*Destructive actions on protected VMs (CUCM, CUBE, base templates, K8s) are strictly denied with `PROTECTED_VM_ACTION_DENIED`.*\n\n### `host_maintenance_shutdown`\n\nFirst request the current plan. This performs live read-only discovery and\nreturns `plan_digest` plus the exact required host confirmation:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"hostAssetId\": \"proxmox-minis-lab\",\n  \"reason\": \"Replace failed cooling fan\",\n  \"operationId\": \"rel-476-maintenance-20260831-01\"\n}\n```\n\nAfter reviewing an eligible plan, apply the same immutable request:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"hostAssetId\": \"proxmox-minis-lab\",\n  \"reason\": \"Replace failed cooling fan\",\n  \"operationId\": \"rel-476-maintenance-20260831-01\",\n  \"apply\": true,\n  \"confirmHostAssetId\": \"proxmox-minis-lab\",\n  \"planDigest\": \"<64-character digest from the dry run>\"\n}\n```\n\nProxmox apply verifies a standalone, fully drained node before requesting the\nsupported node shutdown. ESXi apply verifies a fully drained host, enters\nmaintenance mode without evacuation, then requests shutdown with `force=false`.\nBoth require physical power-on for recovery; ESXi remains in maintenance mode\nuntil an operator verifies the host and exits it. Start CUCM publishers first,\nverify replication, then start subscribers.\n\n### 7. `appliance_exec`\nExecute mediated Call Telemetry appliance CLI operations over secure SSH on Port 2222 with output sanitization and secret redaction.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"targetIp\": \"192.168.124.105\",             // appliance IP or hostname\n  \"port\": 2222,                              // SSH port (default: 2222)\n  \"action\": \"db_vacuum\",                     // \"status\" | \"health\" | \"diag\" | \"db_vacuum\" | \"migrate\" | \"docker\" | \"logs\" | \"version\"\n  \"args\": {\n    \"table\": \"cdr_records\",\n    \"vacuumFull\": true,\n    \"vacuumAnalyze\": true\n  },\n  \"timeoutMs\": 30000\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Client Configuration\n\nCredentials are inherited from the process environment. Do not put a Doppler\ntoken, Proxmox credential, ESXi password, or appliance password in checked-in\nMCP JSON. A GitHub token authorizes repository fetches only; it is not a\nDoppler token and cannot authenticate a hypervisor.\n\nFor stdio clients, credential failure is diagnostic-first: MCP initialization,\ntool discovery, and `credential_status` remain available, while every\noperational tool returns `CREDENTIALS_UNAVAILABLE`. The server never invents a\nmock transport. Repair the direct provider credentials or the config-scoped\n`DOPPLER_TOKEN`, then restart the client. SSE startup remains fail-closed.\n\n### Governed Call Telemetry workspace\n\nUse the `ct-lab` plugin and its `launch-ct-lab-mcp.sh` entrypoint. Cursor\ndiscovers the plugin-owned `.mcp.json`; do not add another `ct-lab` entry to\nthe workspace `.cursor/mcp.json`, because that would start two servers. The\nlauncher pins the reviewed package version and inherits credentials from the\nagent environment without invoking an interactive login.\n\n### Claude Desktop Configuration\n\nFor a standalone, non-plugin installation, add the server to\n`claude_desktop_config.json` and start Claude Desktop from an environment that\nalready contains either direct lab credentials or a config-scoped\n`DOPPLER_TOKEN`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"mcpServers\": {\n    \"ct-lab\": {\n      \"command\": \"npx\",\n      \"args\": [\"-y\", \"@calltelemetry/ct-lab-mcp@0.7.1\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Cursor Configuration\n\nFor a standalone repository that does not install the Call Telemetry plugin,\nthe equivalent project configuration is below. Start Cursor from a terminal or\nsupervisor that already exports `DOPPLER_TOKEN` (or the complete direct lab\ncredential set) so the `npx` child inherits it. If a desktop launcher does not\ninherit that environment, configure the environment in the operating-system\nlauncher or supervisor\u2014not in checked-in `.cursor/mcp.json`.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"mcpServers\": {\n    \"ct-lab\": {\n      \"command\": \"npx\",\n      \"args\": [\"-y\", \"@calltelemetry/ct-lab-mcp@0.7.1\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Programmatic TypeScript Usage\n\n```typescript\nimport { createMcpServer, startStdioServer, SlotManager, MultiHypervisorManager } from \"@calltelemetry/ct-lab-mcp\";\n\nconst server = createMcpServer({\n  name: \"custom-lab-mcp\",\n  version: \"1.0.0\",\n});\n\nawait startStdioServer(server);\n```\n\n---\n\n## Development & Verification\n\n```bash\n# Typecheck TypeScript source\nnpm run typecheck\n\n# Run full Vitest test suite\nnpm test\n\n# Build production distribution to dist/\nnpm run build\n\n# Verify npm bundle package\nnpm pack --dry-run\n```\n\n---\n\n## License\n\nMIT \u00a9 2026 [Call Telemetry](https://calltelemetry.com) / Contributors\n",
